      "cite": "inverse-2x2",
      "prompt": "<p>Solve $\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] \\kvecx = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$ by using matrix inverse.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "multi",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "parts": [
          {
            "part": "x₁",
            "value": -4
          },
          {
            "part": "x₂",
            "value": 4.5
          }
        ],
        "solution": "The determinant is $1 \\cdot 4 - 2 \\cdot 3 = -2 \\neq 0$, so the matrix is invertible, and the $2 \\times 2$ formula gives $\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]^{-1} = \\frac{1}{-2}\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} -2 & 1 \\\\ 3/2 & -1/2 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$. Then $\\kvecx = \\kAinv \\kvecb = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} -2 & 1 \\\\ 3/2 & -1/2 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} -10 + 6 \\\\ 15/2 - 3 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} -4 \\\\ 9/2 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$. Check: $-4 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} = 5$ and $3(-4) + 4 \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} = 6$."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "e3",
      "source_id": "3.2.3",
      "source_number": "3.2.3",
      "kind": "exercise",
      "bloom": "Apply",
