      "cite": "thm-3-2-1",
      "prompt": "<p>For which $h$ is $\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 8 & h \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$ not invertible? Is there only one such $h$? Are there several? Infinitely many?</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "number",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "value": 9,
        "solution": "By Theorem 3.2.1 the matrix fails to be invertible exactly when its determinant is zero. Expanding along the first row, $\\det = 1(5h - 48) - 2(4h - 42) + 3(32 - 35) = -3h + 27 = -3(h - 9)$. This is zero only for $h = 9$: there is exactly one such $h$. (With $h = 9$ it is the matrix whose determinant the text computes to be $0$.)"
      }
