      "cite": "rref",
      "prompt": "<p>Solve $\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 5 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 3 & 3 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] \\kvecx = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$.</p>",
      "answer": {
        "type": "open",
        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "There are infinitely many solutions: $\\kxthree$ is free, and $\\kxone = -2 + 4\\kxthree$, $\\kxtwo = 4 - 9\\kxthree$. The augmented matrix row reduces to the reduced row echelon form $\\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 0 & -4 & | & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 9 & | & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & | & 0 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$: the third column has no leading entry, so $\\kxthree$ is a free variable, and the first two rows say $\\kxone - 4\\kxthree = -2$ and $\\kxtwo + 9\\kxthree = 4$. (The matrix has determinant $0$, so it is not invertible, and the right-hand side happens to give a consistent system.) For example $\\kxthree = 0$ gives $\\kvecx = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} -2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$: $-10 + 12 = 2$, $-16 + 16 = 0$, $-12 + 12 = 0$."
      }
