        "generated_by": "ai",
        "solution": "For example $\\kA = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$, $\\kB = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$, $\\kC = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right]$. Then $\\kA\\kB = \\left[ \\begin{smallmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{smallmatrix} \\right] = \\kA\\kC$, since the zero second row of $\\kA$ never looks at the second row of $\\kB$ or $\\kC$, where they differ, yet $\\kB \\neq \\kC$. Any such $\\kA$ must be singular: were it invertible, multiplying by $\\kAinv$ on the left would give $\\kB = \\kC$."
